.TH "DNSTOOL" "1" "October 2026" "dnstool 0.1.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
dnstool \- look up DNS records against internal and external resolvers
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]dnstool\f[R] [\f[B]\-\-nameserver\f[R] \f[I]ADDRESS\f[R]]
\f[I]hostname\f[R]
.PP
\f[B]dnstool\f[R] [\f[B]\-h\f[R]|\f[B]\-\-help\f[R]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\f[B]dnstool\f[R] performs a DNS lookup for \f[I]hostname\f[R] and
prints the results twice: once as resolved by the system\(cqs configured
resolver (labelled \f[I]internal\f[R]), and once as resolved by an
external nameserver (labelled \f[I]external\f[R]).
Running both side by side makes it easy to spot split\-horizon DNS,
stale caches, or records that differ between a private zone and the
public internet.
.PP
If \f[I]hostname\f[R] parses as an IPv4 or IPv6 address,
\f[B]dnstool\f[R] performs a reverse lookup instead, querying the
\f[B]PTR\f[R] record for the corresponding \f[I]in\-addr.arpa\f[R] or
\f[I]ip6.arpa\f[R] name.
.PP
Otherwise a forward lookup is performed, querying the following record
types: \f[B]A\f[R], \f[B]AAAA\f[R], \f[B]MX\f[R], \f[B]TXT\f[R],
\f[B]NS\f[R], \f[B]CNAME\f[R], and \f[B]SOA\f[R].
Record types with no answer are omitted from the output.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\f[B]\-\-nameserver\f[R] \f[I]ADDRESS\f[R]
IP address of the external nameserver to query for the second lookup.
Defaults to \f[I]8.8.8.8\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]\-h\f[R], \f[B]\-\-help\f[R]
Print a usage summary and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
\f[B]dnstool\f[R] exits 0 on success and 2 if the command line could not
be parsed.
Resolution failures are reported on standard output and do not change
the exit status.
.SH EXAMPLES
Look up all supported records for a hostname:
.IP
.EX
dnstool example.com
.EE
.PP
Reverse look up an IP address:
.IP
.EX
dnstool 8.8.8.8
.EE
.PP
Compare the internal resolver against Cloudflare rather than Google:
.IP
.EX
dnstool example.com \-\-nameserver 1.1.1.1
.EE
